A||N||7||Seanh||Oh No! Broke a Coolent hose today||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||09-26-2000||07:26 PM||||I was comming home tonight and I stoped off to talk to some guys I know. Get back in my car and take, less then a mile down the road my car is overheating. Great. I stop on a side road to take a look. Pop the lid and smoke is pouring off the front exh. manifold. Went for my fire ext. but thankfully didnt need it. The car cooled down and I looked under the engine and theres the puddle of anti-freeze. I parked the car in a driveway and got a ride home. I am planning on going back tonight with a truck to pull it home. My questions are:<P>1. Should I tow the car home (Less then 2 more miles). Or should I drive it once it cools down? If it overheats again I could park it and tow it from there. Is it safe to drive it without it catching fire?<P>2. If I tow it home: I read in the Haynes book that you shouldnt tow a 86GT. Is that right??? I will only need to go about 1 more mile and on residential roads. How should I hook to the car?<P>3. I cant see the brake in it from the top. I havent really looked up at from the bottem either. Could you tell me about what has happened given the above information. What hose am I looking for and how do I replace it?<P>Thanks guys.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hank is Here||09-26-2000||07:43 PM||||I drive tow truck and would say it would be safe for two miles to tow it as long as you keep it under 35 mph.  Going high speeds the tranny does not get proper lube and is when you get into problems.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||09-26-2000||08:50 PM||||Just an update. Thanks Hank. Pulled the car home. I am going to start working on it tomarrow morning. I think I am going to replace the hose and then flush and fill the cooling system. Probally a stupid question, but how much water to anti-freeze ratio? <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||09-26-2000||08:53 PM||||50/50||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||09-26-2000||09:00 PM||||Ok, Thats what I thought. But then again I had a ford that they told me to mix 60/40. Just making sure. <P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||09-26-2000||09:07 PM||||50/50 and 60/40 are both right, it just depends on what kind of anti freexe you are using. It should say on the bottle though.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||09-26-2000||10:53 PM||||welllllll... In theory, it should be 52/48...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||09-27-2000||10:19 AM||||When you refill the coolant be sure and follow the procedure for burping the air out of the system.  Mine is very sensitive to just a little bit of air.<P>I had the gasket between the timing chain cover and block on my 85SE v-6 squeeze out and leak last week.  What a joy to fix.  Anyway after all was done I burped the system while the car was still up on jack stands.  Everything was fine on the test drive that night.<P>The next morning on the way to work the temps start to swing from one extreme to the other.  I go home and swap for my van.  After work I pull the Fiero out to the end of my driveway with the nose down just slightly and re-burp the system.  I was only able to add about a pint of coolant but that was enough to fix the problem.<P>I guess when I burped it with the rear end way up in the air the coolant started running out of the radiator fill before all the air was purged.  Having it more level with the front slightly lower than the rear allowed the air to escape without losing too much coolant.<P>It doesn't take a very large air bubble to air lock the water pump.<P>If your car has standard transmission it shouldn't matter how far you tow it ( as far as I know).  The standard trans lubricant is not cooled by anything other than normal convection.  Without the engine heat it will actually run cooler than if the engine is running.  <P>The fluid drive of an automatic transmission generates heat as the fluid is agitated in the trans.  The trans. relies on the trans. fluid pump to move fluid through the cooler to keep the fluid temps down.  The trans pump is driven by the engine side of the transmission and therefore is not working if the engine is not running (such as when it is being towed).<P>If you are referring the the hose that connects the passenger side coolant pipe to the water pump inlet(located under the RR wheel well) on your 86GT the part number is 10036860 (Rad. Outlet. RR - 85-87).  The hose that goes between the engine and the coolant crossover pipe is part number 10032771 (Rad. Inlet RR - 85-88).  The hose between the crossover pipe and the driver's side coolant pipe (under the drivers side rear wheel well) is  part number 10041086 (Rad. Inlet Inter - 85-87).<P>While I'm at it here are the last two radiator hoses.  The drivers side radiator hose part number is 10024723 (Rad Inlet Frt Hose - 85-87) and the passenger side radiator hose part number is 10024909 (Rad Outlet Frt Hose - 85-87).  (All this is from my 22P manual).<P>I find it easier to replace the hose under the RR wheel well if I remove the RR wheel and remove the splash guard (rubber curtain in the wheel well) and the inner fender liner.  It only takes a few minutes to get them off and it gives much better access the the upper hose clamp.  Removing the lower A/C bracket (three bolts) also gives better access to pulling the hose off the timing chain cover boss.  It can all be done without removing any of this but it will make the job easier.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
